  "final": "I added `src/exporter.py` with `export_rows(rows, format)` and made `export_csv`, `export_json` and the dispatcher's `export` all call it. `python3 checks/public.py` passes, and so does a new `checks/exporter_checks.py`.\n\n- **Shared code:** `export_rows` holds the only row normalization and the CSV and JSON serialization. It keeps only `id` and `name`, uses `''` for missing or `None` values, and stringifies everything else. It builds new dicts and consumes the input once, so one-shot iterables work and the input rows aren't changed.\n- **Output rules:** CSV uses LF line endings, standard quoting, and an `id,name` header even with no rows. JSON uses `ensure_ascii=False` and `separators=(',', ':')`, with no trailing newline, so empty input gives `[]`.\n- **Unsupported formats:** any unsupported format raises `ValueError(\"unsupported format\")`. The check also covers `None` and an unhashable value like `[]`.\n- **Wrappers:** `src/csv_exporter.py` and `src/json_exporter.py` are now one-line wrappers around `export_rows`.\n- **Dispatcher:** `src/dispatcher.py` now delegates straight to `export_rows` instead of choosing between the two wrappers. Results and the error message are unchanged.\n- **New checks:** `checks/exporter_checks.py` tests quoting, non-ASCII text, missing and `None` values, extra keys, unchanged input, iterators and generators, empty input, and bad formats through both entry points.",
